\section*{Project Overview}
ACE-9K (Astrophysics Conservation Engine) is a multi-layer conservation and 
stability architecture designed to ingest multimessenger astrophysical data 
and evaluate conservation laws, force propagation, structural response, and 
topological stability under extreme gradient injection.

This README provides a high-level description of the system, its components, 
invariants, and testing workflow.

\section{System Architecture}
ACE-9K consists of four integrated layers:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{QP-Core} (Quantum--Pressure Core)\\
    Information-density pressure substrate defined by:
    \[
    P(x) = \nabla \rho(x), \qquad 
    \Gamma_P = \frac{\partial}{\partial x^\mu} \log \rho(x^\mu).
    \]

    \item \textbf{SRK} (Structural Resistance Kernel)\\
    Stress--strain response governed by:
    \[
    \sigma_{ij} = C_{ijkl}\epsilon_{kl}, \qquad 
    f_{\text{yield}} = \sqrt{\sigma_{ij}\sigma^{ij}} - \sigma_{\text{crit}}.
    \]

    \item \textbf{FDE} (Force Dynamics Engine)\\
    Dynamic propagation modeled by:
    \[
    M\ddot{x}^\mu + D\dot{x}^\mu + Kx^\mu = F^\mu.
    \]

    \item \textbf{SFM} (Stability--Failure Manifold)\\
    Global stability evaluated via:
    \[
    \lambda_{\max}(K - \Gamma_P) < 0.
    \]
\end{enumerate}

